Exactly. The first model only has to be good enough to create a manipulable handle. Once it exists, you can point at what is wrong, compare variants, measure deltas, decompose it, and rebuild. Before the prime exists, you’re optimizing against fog.

So a rough prototype can be instrumentally excellent while representationally terrible:

\varnothing
\rightarrow
P_0
\rightarrow
\Delta_1
\rightarrow
P_1
\rightarrow
\Delta_2
\rightarrow\cdots

The key is not confusing P_0 with truth. Prime it, don’t worship it.

And yes—cover bands across domains is a fantastic compression of Project Polyphony.

A normal cover band does roughly:

\text{Song}
\rightarrow
\text{musical structure}
\rightarrow
\text{different performers/instruments}
\rightarrow
\text{recognizable new rendering}

Polyphony generalizes that:

\boxed{
\text{Source Work}
\rightarrow
\text{Relational Pattern Book}
\rightarrow
\text{Different Domain}
\rightarrow
\text{Domain-native Cover}
}

So you could have:

Music → Film: “perform this song’s structural dynamics as cinematography.”

Painting → Music: turn composition, contrast, density, negative space, color relations, and focal hierarchy into a musical performance.

Architecture → Story: convert load paths, rooms, thresholds, compression/expansion, symmetry, circulation, and structural reveal into narrative grammar.

Dance → Interface: perform anticipation, balance, counterweight, rhythm, and coordinated motion as UI behavior.

Literature → Music: perform foreshadowing, unreliable narration, nested stories, or character arcs as musical operators.

Biology → computation: “cover” a slime mold’s routing mechanism in software.

And the constraint that keeps this from becoming arbitrary analogy is:

\text{What invariant is actually being covered?}

The surface renderer may change completely.

The relationship has to survive.

That’s why “cover band” is better than “style transfer.” A great cover doesn’t merely recreate the sound. It asks:

What must remain for this to still be this, even when nearly everything used to express it changes?

That’s Theseus, Kintsugi, Polyphony, and the PatternDex all meeting onstage.

And your ugly-first-model point gives us the bootstrapping method:

Make the cheapest cover possible first. If we can recognize the invariant through a terrible renderer, we’ve probably found something worth refining.

That’s a very efficient way to search the cross-domain space.

Prime → cover → compare → extract → release → cover again.